## 1. Introduction
TS-1 as a representative zeolite with the skeleton structure which is composed of silicon-oxygen tetrahedron and titaniumoxygen tetrahedron, is first synthesized by isomorphously substituting part of the skeleton Si atom in silicate-1 with Ti atom isomorphism in 1983[1]. The existence of titanium species in TS-1 mainly includes four-coordinate framework titanium, highcoordination active titanium (mainly $\mathrm{TiO}_{6}$ ) and six-coordinate anatase[2,3,4]. And the framework titanium is generally recognized as the catalytically active center which can activate $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ under mild conditions, performing excellent catalytic performance for partial catalytic oxidation reactions such as phenol oxidation and alkene epoxidation[5,6,7,8,9]. However, due to the mismatch of hydrolysis rate between traditional titanium source and silicon source, a large amount of anatase $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ is easily generated in the preparation process. And those anatase species usually cover some active sites in TS-1 and lead to ineffective decomposition of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ , thus creating negative effects for the catalyst activity[10,11]. In addition, the limitation of the micropore diameter of ten-membered ring (about $0.55~\mathrm{{nm}}$ ) not only restrict mass transportation, but also affect the accessibility of the Ti active sites for larger molecules (such as alkylbenzenes, cycloolefins, etc.)[12,13,14]. So the synthesis of anatase-free TS-1 with hierarchical pore structure is of great significance and highly desirable for boosting its performance.
In terms of eliminating the anatase phase in TS-1, the formation of anatase phase can be effectively suppressed during the synthesis process by adjusting synthesis parameters, changing titanium source and so on. Zhao et al.[15] prepared anatase-free TS-1 zeolite by carefully controlling the synthesis parameters during the entire crystallization process. Gao and coworkers[16] proposed that replacing TBOT with ${\mathrm{TiCl}}_{3}$ as titanium precursor could avoid the formation of anatase owing to $\mathrm{Ti}^{3+}$ has a greater tendency to be uniformly dispersed. In addition, rotational crystallization strategy is also a feasible way to eliminate anatase by balancing the incorporation rate of Ti and Si into the framework[17]. As for constructing hierarchical TS-1, lots of efforts have been made, and common strategies mainly contain: (1) the chemical etching post-treatment approach[18,19,20], generally refers the desilication method with strong alkali or organic alkali treatment[21,22]; (2) the template strategies[23,24,25], includ the hard-templating method using partial carbonaceous templates like carbon nanoparticles[26,27] and soft-templating method using structure-directing agents such as organosilane and surfactant[28,29,30]; (3) mesoporogen-free approaches, involves the SAC route dominated by the solid phase transition mechanism[31,32] and zeolite crystal seeds route[33].
Although significant efforts have been made to eliminate anatase or construct hierarchical pores in TS-1 independently, there are very few report about fabricating hierarchical TS-1 without anatase [33,34,35]. Very recently, Yu et al. proposed the hierarchical TS-1 can be prepared with a novel two-step hydrothermal synthesis method using tetrabutylammonium hydroxide (TBAOH) as the sole template[35], successfully constructed hierarchical TS-1 zeolite structure and avoided the formation of anatase species in the zeolite crystals at the same time. It is still highly desirable yet challenging to develop a facile and effective synthesis method to achieve synchronous construction of hierarchical TS-1 zeolite free of anatase.
Metal organic frameworks (MOFs) is a type of threedimensional porous crystalline materials formed by selfassembly of metal atoms or clusters and organic ligands through coordination bond interactions[36,37,38]. As one of the most attractive member of the MOFs family, Ti-based MOFs are often applied for photocatalytic field owing to the outstanding chemical stability and redox activity[39,40,41]. But there has never been a related report using Ti-based MOFs to synthesize TS-1. In this work, we demonstrated a novel MOFs-induced $(\mathsf{N H}_{2}-\mathsf{M I L}-125$ ) strategy to prepare anatase-free hierarchical TS-1 by adopting two different synthetic routes. The roles and advantages of MOFs in eliminating anatase and creating hierarchical pores were proved by a variety of characterizations, and the detailed structural evolution processes and crystal growth mechanisms for two routes were also investigated. Furthermore, the obtained two series TS-1 catalysts showed excellent catalytic activity in 1 hexene epoxidation compared to the conventional microporous TS-1 zeolite, and the representative samples all possessed a favorable reusability.
## 2. Experimental section
## 2.1. Chemicals and materials
The raw materials used in this work include Titanium (IV) isopropoxide (TTIP, $99\%$ , J&K Scientific Co.), 2-aminoterephthalic acid $(\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{B D C-N H}_{2}$ , $98\%$ , Shanghai Aladdin Reagents Co., ltd), tetraethoxysilane (TEOS, $99\ \%$ Sinopharm Chemical Reagent Co., ltd), tetrabutyl titanate (TBOT, $99\%$ , J&K Scientific Co.), tetrapropylammonium hydroxide (TPAOH, $25\ \mathrm{wt\%}$ , J&K Scientific Co.), $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ $30\ \mathrm{wt\%}$ , Sinopharm Chemical Reagent Co., ltd), 1 hexene ( $99\ \%$ ， Beijing Innochem Scientific Co., ltd), $\mathsf{N},N.$ -dimethylformamide (DMF, $99\%$ , Beijing Tong Guang Fine Chemicals Company), methanol $99\%$ Beijing Tong Guang Fine Chemicals Company) and acetonitrile ( $99\ \%$ , Sinopharm Chemical Reagent Co., ltd). All used reagents were without further purification.
## 2.2. Synthesis of anatase-free hierarchical MTS
The $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL-125 as titanium source was pre-synthesized by a conventional method[42]. A series of MTS zeolites with different titanium content were prepared when the molar composition of raw materials was 1.0 TEOS: 0.4 TPAOH: $20\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ : n Ti within MOFs $\mathbf{\dot{n}}=0.025$ , 0,030, 0.035, 0.040), and were labelled as MTS-0.025, MTS-0.030, MTS-0.035 and MTS-0.040, respectively. As a typical procedure: Different amounts of MOFs were added into a mixed solution consisting of appropriate amount of TPAOH and deionized water under stirring conditions. When the solution becomes clear, it was transferred to a $25~\mathrm{mL}$ test tube containing TEOS for precursor hydrolysis. The whole hydrolysis process included the room temperature stirring of 2 h $(25^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ and the high temperature stirring of $^{4\mathrm{~h~}}$ $\left(80^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\right)$ . And during this process, it was necessary to add appropriate amount of deionized water several times to maintain the water content of original system. Then the hydrolysis product was transferred into the hydrothermal autoclave and crystallized at $170^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for 2 days under static conditions. Finally, the white solids obtained after filtration were washed with deionized water three times and dried at $80^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in a vacuum drying oven overnight, followed by calcination at $550^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{6\mathrm{~h~}}$ .
## 2.3. Synthesis of anatase-free hierarchical MTS-SAC
A series of MTS-SAC zeolites with different titanium content were prepared when the molar composition of raw materials was 1.0 TEOS: 0.2 TPAOH: $20~\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ : n Ti within MOFs $\mathbf{\tilde{n}_{\lambda}}=\mathbf{\tilde{0.025}}$ 0,030, 0.035, 0.040), and were labelled as MTS-SAC-0.025, MTSSAC-0.030, MTS-SAC-0.035 and MTS-SAC-0.040, respectively. As a typical procedure: Different amounts of MOFs were added into a mixed solution consisting of appropriate amount of TPAOH and deionized water under stirring conditions. When the solution becomes clear, it was transferred into the $25{\mathrm{~mL~}}$ three-necked flasks containing TEOS for precursor hydrolysis. After stirring for $^{2\mathrm{~h~}}$ at room temperature, the mixed solution were heated up to $80~^{\circ}C$ and stirred until the liquid phase components were completely volatilized. Then the obtained dry gel was grinded into powder and transferred into a $5~\mathrm{mL}$ open Teflon liner. Then $5~\mathrm{mL}$ Teflon liner was placed into another $100~\mathrm{{mL}}$ Teflon-lined stainless steel autoclave cotaining $4~\mathrm{mL}$ distilled $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ (There was no contact between water and dry gel) and heated at $170^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for 2 days under static conditions. Finally, the white solids obtained after filtration were washed with deionized water three times and dried at 80 $\mathrm{{}^{\circ}C}$ in a vacuum drying oven overnight, followed by calcination at $550^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{6\mathrm{~h~}}$ .
## 2.4. Synthesis of conventional TS-1 (TS-C)
Conventional TS-1 were synthesized with adding TBOT as titanium source, and the molar composition of raw materials was 1.0 TEOS: 0.4 TPAOH: $20~\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ : 0.025 TBOT. TBOT and TEOS were first mixed and then added into the solution consisting of TPAOH and deionized water under stirring condition [43]. The rest of the steps were basically same as the above-mentioned preparation process of MTS. Finally, the white products were labelled as TS-C.
## 2.5. Characterization
The crystal structure and phase composition of samples were obtainded by the powder $\mathsf{X}$ -ray powder diffraction instrument (M21X) under the conditions of 40 KV, 40 mA and $5^{\circ}<2\uptheta<35^{\circ}$ The textural properties such as specific surface area and pore volume of samples were examined on a Autosorb-IQ automatic static physical adsorption instrument after the samples were degassed at $300^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for 6 h under vacuum. The morphology and crystal size were observed by the scanning electron microscopy (SEM) using a ZEISS SUPRA 55. Transmission electron microscopy (TEM) images were obtained by a JEM-2100F. Fourier transform infrared spectra (FTIR) were carried out on a Thermal Nicolet 6700 type Fouriertransform infrared spectrometer. The UV–vis diffuse reflectance spectra were tested by using a SHIMADZU UV-2550 UV–vis absorption spectrometer. The X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) analysis of catalyst was carried out by a Thermo ESCALAB 250Xi XPS spectrometer. UV Raman spectra ( $325~\mathrm{{nm}}$ of excitation wavelength) were recorded by the raman spectrometer of Horiba LabRAMHREvolution. The solid nuclear magnetic resonance $^{13}{\mathsf{C}}$ and $^{29}\mathrm{Si}$ MAS NMR) was tested with a 500 MHz Avance III 600WB spectrometer (Bruker, Germany).
## 2.6. Catalytic tests
The epoxidation of 1 hexene with $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ was performed within a $25~\mathrm{mL}$ three neck glass flask under the conditions of $313\ \mathrm{K},$ , reflux condensation and continuous stirring at atmospheric pressure. In a typical run, $8.496~\mathrm{mL}$ of methanol solvent, $10.5\mathrm{mmol}$ of 1-hexene, $10.92\ \mathrm{mmol}$ of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ $(30~\mathrm{wt\%})$ and $88.2~\mathrm{mg}$ of the catalyst were added to the flask (the reaction ratio of substrate to hydrogen peroxide was 1: 1.04), and the mixture was required to react for $^{4\mathrm{~h~}}$
The liquid supernatant was separated by centrifugation and analyzed with the gas chromatograph (Agilent 7890A) which equipped with a $30\mathrm{m}$ capillary column (HP-5MS) and an FID detector when the reaction was finished. All the products were confirmed by GC– MS (Agilent 5975C), and the conversion of substrate (C) and the selectivity of epoxides (S) were estimated according to the follow formulas:
$$
C=\stackrel{(n_{0}-n_{1})}{n_{0}}\times100\%
$$
$$
S=\frac{n_{e p o}}{(n_{e p o}+n_{b y p})}\times100\%
$$
n0, n1, nepo and $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{n_{bpy}}}$ represent the initial amount of reaction substrate, the residual amount of substrate after reacting for $^{4\mathrm{~h~}}$ ， the amount of epoxidized product and the total amount of other by-products, respectively. As for the recycling tests of the 1 hexene epoxidation reaction, the used catalyst was separated by centrifugation, dried at $353\mathrm{K}$ overnight and calcined in air at $823\mathrm{K}$ for $6\mathrm{{h}}$ for the next cycle.
## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1. Characterization of MTS and MTS-SAC zeolites
Anatase-free hierarchical MTS and MTS-SAC were synthesized using a Ti-based MOFs $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL-125 as dual-functional template by traditional hydrothermal synthesis and SAC method, respectively. The general synthesis processes of the two preparation routes are shown in Scheme 1. For comparison, conventional TSC was also synthesized by hydrothermal preparation process using TBOT as titanium source instead of $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL-125.
Fig. 1a shows the XRD patterns of MTS gradient samples synthesized using different amounts of $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL-125 precursor. All the curves show the sharp characteristic peaks at $7.9^{\circ}$ , $8.9^{\circ}$ , $23.0^{\circ}$ , $23.9^{\circ}$ and $24.4^{\circ}$ , which are consistent with the MFI topological structure, indicating the TS-1 with high crystallinity were successfully synthesized by MOFs-induced strategy [3,6,8]. It should be noted that no characteristic peak of anatase phase at $25.3^{\circ}$ were observed for all the MTS samples, suggesting the generation of the anatase $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ was efficiently restricted.
The UV–vis spectroscopy is a common strategy to distinguish the titanium species in the framework, so the MTS were also characterized for detection of the Ti coordination states of TS-1 zeolites. Owing to the transition of 2p electrons of the oxygen atom in the TS-1 framework to the 3d empty orbit of the four-coordinated framework $\mathrm{Ti}^{4+}$ ions, an absorption peak at around $220~\mathrm{{nm}}$ can be discovered. In addition, the absorption peak at $320\mathrm{nm}$ is attributed to the non-framework titanium species, and mainly in the form of anatase $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ [9,12]. According to the Fig. 1c, the TS-C sample prepared with conversional method exhibits a strong absorption peak at $320\ \mathrm{nm}$ , indicating the existence of considerable amounts of anatase species. All MTS samples synthesized with different addition of MOFs only show a strong absorption peak at $220\mathrm{nm}$ , indicating that the Ti atoms in these samples mainly exist in the four-coordinate state and the formation of the anatase phase were suppressed during the hydrothermal synthesis process. The UV-Raman was also used to investigate the existent forms of Ti species in the MTS samples under the conditions of excitation light wavelengths of $325~\mathrm{{nm}}$ . As shown in Fig. 1e, all the MTS samples display vibration peaks at 380, 810, 965 and $1125~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , which are corresponding to the characteristic vibration peaks of Si-O-Si in the MFI topology structure (380 and $810~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ ) and the stretching vibration peak of Ti-O-Si species (965 and $1125~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ ), respectively. While the TS-C sample shows obviously different absorption peaks sited at 395, 515 and $632~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , which are attributed to the presence of anatase $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ [17,26]. The UV-Raman results proved that using MOFs as Ti precursor can effectively avoid the formation of anatase phase, which is consistent with the UV–vis spectra results. Based on the above XRD, UV–vis and UV-Raman results, it can be concluded that Ti based MOFs induced strategy is an effective way to obtain anatase-free TS-1.
The FT-IR spectrum (Fig. 1g) were characterized to investigate the level of titanium incorporation in the framework of the MTS products. Generally, there are three obvious characteristic absorption peaks around at 800, 960 and $1100\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ at low wave numbers of spectra. The peaks appearing at 800 and $1100~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ correspond to the symmetrical and antisymmetrical stretching vibration of the silica tetrahedron in the framework, belonging to the characteristic diffraction peaks of the MFI structure. The absorption band at $960\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ can be attributed to the Si-O-Ti stretching vibration peak caused by the framework titanium[23,28,30]. To evaluate the content of framework Ti, the relative intensity of the band at $960\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ to that of $800~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ $(I_{960}/I_{800})$ is used, and this value is usually positively correlated with the content of framework Ti. According to the Fig. 1g, the value of $\pmb{I_{960}}/\pmb{I_{800}}$ gradually increases with the addition amount of Ti MOFs (from 1.45 to 1.97), indicating that the content of framework Ti in the MTS sample gradually increased.
Fig. 1b, d, f, h show the XRD, UV–vis, UV-Raman and FT-IR patterns of MTS-SAC synthesized using different amounts of $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL125 precursor, respectively. Similar to MTS samples, all the MTSSAC samples also show the sharp XRD characteristic peaks of the typical MFI-type structure and no obvious amorphous peaks or characteristic peak of anatase $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ (Fig. 1b); the UV–vis spectra only exhibit an absorption band at $210\ \mathrm{nm}$ (Fig. 1d); the UVRaman spectra display vibration peaks consistent with MTS-1 at 380, 810, 965 and $1125~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , and no obvious anatase absorption peak can be found (Fig. 1f). As for the FT-IR results, peaks around at 800, 960 and $1100\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ can also be found at low wave numbers of spectra. When the titanium-silicon molar ratio in the precursor $_{\mathrm{i}s<0.035}$ , the relative intensity value of $\pmb{I_{960}}/\pmb{I_{800}}$ gradually increases with the addition amount of Ti MOFs (from 1.94 to 2.27), proving that the content of framework Ti in the synthesized sample also gradually increased. The MTS-SAC-0.040 has a same I960/800 value with the MTS-SAC-0.035, which may be due to the saturation of the framework titanium content. In addition, all the MTS-SAC samples show higher $\pmb{I_{960}}/\pmb{I_{800}}$ values than MTS samples, indicating that the SAC process may be more conducive to the introduction of Ti into the framework. The above results further confirms that MOFs-induced strategy is an effective and universal way to fabricate anatase-free TS-1.
In order to further distinguish the surface composition and Ti coordination states of the samples, the XPS spectra of the obtained MTS zeolites were analyzed and shown in Fig. 2a-b. In the C1s high-resolution XPS spectrum (Fig. 2a), peak at $284.8\ \mathrm{~eV}$ corresponds to the electronic binding energy of instrument carbon. And a weak absorption peak symbolizing carbon in $C{\mathrm{-}}C$ species can be found at the binding energy of $288.9\ \mathrm{eV}$ representing the presence of a small quantity of unsintered $\mathrm{TPA}^{+}[44]$ . In Fig. 2b, all the MTS samples only possess an obvious Ti $2\mathsf{p}_{3/2}$ peak at $460.3\ \mathrm{eV}$ , which can be attributed to the electronic binding energy of tetrahedrally coordinated titanium species $\mathrm{Ti}{-}0_{4}$ species), indicating that Ti mainly exists in the framework of MTS-1 products [27,43]. However, in terms of TS-C, there is another Ti $2\mathsf{p}_{3/2}$ peak can be found at $458.4\ \mathrm{eV}$ , suggesting the existence of anatase phase[17,35]. Combined with the results of the previous XRD, UV–vis and UV-Raman tests, using Ti based MOFs as the precursor to synthesize TS-1 can effectively avoid the formation of anatase phase. The XPS spectra of the obtained MTS-SAC products were also analyzed and shown in Fig S3a-b. In the Fig S3a (C1s spectrum), similar to MTS samples, the binding energy values of 284.8 eV and $288.9\ \mathrm{eV}$ are attributed to the instrument carbon and unsintered $\mathrm{TPA}^{+}$ . All the samples only possess an evident binding energy value of $460.3\:\mathrm{eV}$ are assigned to the Ti $2\mathrm{p}_{3/2}$ photoelectrons of tetrahedrally coordinated framework Ti- ${{\cdot}}_{0_{4}}$ species, demonstrating that the inexistence of anatase phase (Fig S3b). Combined with the results of the XRD, UV–vis and UV-Raman tests, TS-1 zeolites without anatase can also be obtained with MOFs as a titanium source by the SAC process.
The $^{13}{\mathsf{C}}$ MAS NMR analysis of MTS-0.025 before calcination is shown in Fig. 2c, the peaks at around 17 ppm, 11 ppm and 63 ppm correspond to the three saturated carbon atoms in the propyl group of the TPAOH template[45]. In addition, a weak absorption peak at 110 ppm which represents aromatic carbon species can be observed, indicating that partial MOFs decomposition products containing benzene ring are anchored in TS-1 zeolite matrix during the crystallization process[46]. The residue ligands play the role for construction of hierarchical pores during calcination process. Fig. 2d is the $^{29}\mathrm{Si}$ MAS NMR spectra of the calcined MTS0.025 wherein the signals at about $-103~\mathrm{ppm}$ and 113 ppm represent the Q3 defective Si species $(\mathsf{S i O})_{3}\mathsf{S i O H},$ and Q4 framework Si species $((\mathrm{SiO})_{4}\mathrm{Si}^{\cdot}$ , respectively[2]. The unconspicuous Q3 absorption peak suggests that the synthesized MTS-0.025 has a relatively pure phase and only contains a few defective sites. In addition, the weak shoulder peak between $-113\mathrm{ppm}$ and $-117$ ppm is usually served as strong evidence for introducing Ti into the zeolite framework[30].
After confirming that MTS and MTS-SAC series zeolites without extra-framework anatase $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ were successfully synthesized, the microstructure and pore structure of these samples was further investigated. The SEM images of several MTS zeolites are shown in Fig. 3a, c, e, g. All the synthesized MTS exhibit regular and uniform coffin shape with size of about $100{-}150~\mathrm{{nm}}$ which is similar with TS-C (Fig S4a). It is worth noting that, there are almost no change of the size of these zeolite particles as the increase of the addition amount of MOFs precursor. This phenonmenon may be caused by the relatively large dosage of structure directing agent TPAOH, leading to higher nucleation rate and a smaller particle size. Combining the TEM images of these samples, compared with TS-C (Fig S4b), the TEM images of all MTS samples show a certain number of intercrystalline mesoporous structures (Fig. 3b, d, f, h).
The morphology of several MTS-SAC samples synthesized by SAC method are shown in Fig. 3i, k, m, o. Unlike MTS samples with a smooth coffin morphology and uniform particle size, all the SAC zeolites possess a wider particle size distribution and irregular rough surface. Moreover, the particle sizes gradually increase form $100-$ $200\ \mathrm{nm}$ of MTS-SAC-0.025 to $500{-}600~\mathrm{nm}$ of MTS-SAC-0.040. This phenomenon may be caused by the following reasons: 1) Compared with the traditional hydrothermal method, minor TPAOH amount makes a lower zeolite nucleation rate during the crystallization process, which is conducive to the growth of crystals; 2) The use of MOFs precursor would consume the $\mathsf{O H}^{-}$ of system and lower the pH value, leading to an increased particle size. Fig. 3j, l, n, p show the corresponding TEM images of MTS-SAC samples, the presented particle size is consistent with the SEM images. But unlike the hydrothermal MTS samples, SAC samples are mainly composed of many nanocrystals with a particle size of a few tens of nanometers, and more obvious intercrystalline pores can be observed.
Fig. 4 shows the $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ adsorption–desorption isotherms and pore size distributions of MTS, MTS-SAC and TS-C samples. In contrast to microporous TS-C with the type-I isotherm, all the MTS samples possess typical type IV adsorption isotherm based on the IUPAC classification, showing the feature of mesoporous materials. Furthermore, a high nitrogen adsorption capacity at $\mathrm{P}/\mathrm{P}_{0}<0.01$ representing the existence of microporous structure can be found, indicating that those samples also contain a lot of micropores. The obvious saturated absorption platform at $\mathrm{P}/\mathrm{P}_{0}>0.95$ demonstrates those MTS zeolites equip the H2 type hysteresis loop and possess a relatively uniform pore size distribution (Fig. 4a). The textural properties of several MTS samples and TS-C are summarized in Table 1. With the increase of the amount of MOFs precursor in the synthesis system, the micropore volume $\mathsf{V}_{\mathrm{Mic}}$ (around $0.16~\mathrm{cm}^{3}/\mathrm{g})$ and mesopore volume $\mathsf{V}_{\mathrm{Mes}}$ (around $0.4\ \mathrm{cm}^{3}/\mathrm{g})$ of the prepared MTS zeolites is basically consistent, suggesting that the MOFs amount has no significant effect on the pore structure of the material. TS-C displays a similar $\mathsf{V}_{\mathrm{Mic}}$ value $(0.15~\mathrm{cm}^{3}/\mathrm{g})$ and $\mathsf{V}_{\mathrm{Mes}}$ value $(0.12~\mathrm{cm}^{3}/\mathrm{g})$ , so the specific surface area is lower $(416.28\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g})$ than MTS samples. In Fig. 4b, only MTS samples have the large mesopores within the range of $20{-}50~\mathrm{nm}$ , indicating that using MOFs as titanium source can also introduce additional hierarchical pore structure.
As for MTS-SAC series samples. In contrast to MTS-SAC-0.035 and MTS-SAC-0.04 showing a type-I adsorption–desorption isotherms, MTS-SAC-0.025 and MTS-SAC-0.030 exhibit the type-IV isotherms with a H3 type hysteresis loop, indicating the presence of obvious mesoporous structure (Fig. 4c). According to Table 1, with the increase of the amount of MOFs precursor in the synthesis system, the $\mathsf{V}_{\mathrm{Mic}}$ value (around $0.12~\mathrm{cm}^{3}/\mathrm{g})$ of all the prepared MTS-SAC zeolites is basically concordant, but the specific surface area is gradually decreasing (from $437.63\ \mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ to $391.91~\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g})$ . And only MTS-SAC-0.025 and MTS-SAC-0.030 have obvious $\mathrm{V}_{\mathrm{Mes}}$ value $(0.33~\mathrm{cm}^{3}/\mathrm{g}$ and $0.31~\mathrm{cm}^{3}/\mathrm{g})$ . From Fig. 4d, MTS-SAC-0.035 and MTS-SAC-0.040 basically have no mesoporous structure similar with the hydrothermal MTS samples. For the MTS-SAC-0.025 and MTS-SAC-0.030, in addition to the mesopore distribution between 20 and $50\ \mathrm{nm}$ , a considerable macroporous structure $\left(>50\ \mathrm{nm}\right)$ also can be found. All those results can indicate that using MOFs as titanium source can introduce additional hierarchical pore structure during the synthesis of TS-1 regardless of hydrothermal synthesis route or SAC process.
## 3.2. Crystallization processes and mechanism of MTS and MTS-SAC zeolites
In order to investigate the formation process of prepared hierarchical MTS and MTS-SAC samples, the micro-morphology images of MTS-0.025 and MTS-SAC-0.025 prepared at different crystallization time were investigated in detail (Fig. 5a-h, j-q and Fig S5a-b). For the MTS hydrothermal samples, after $0.5\mathrm{~h~}$ of crystallization, the precursor was found to be transformed into many small particles with a particle size of several nanometers to tens of nanometers. Combining with the corresponding XRD results (Figure. S6a), the sample crystallized for $0.5\mathrm{~h~}$ already possesses a typical MFI topology, indicating that the nucleation induction period has finished. With the crystallization time further extended to $^\textrm{\scriptsize1h}$ , the primary nanocrys-talline particles start oriented aggregation (Fig. 5b, f). Partial zeolite particles with certain size and shape are initially formed after crystallization $^{2\mathrm{h}}$ (Fig. 5c, g). By comparison with the sample crystallized for $^{48\mathrm{~h~}}$ (Fig. 3a, b), it can be found that the zeolite particles have grown completely when the crystallization time is $24\mathrm{~h~}$ (Fig. 5d, h). Fig S6a shows the corresponding XRD curves of MTS samples prepared with different crystallization time $(0.5\mathrm{~h},1\mathrm{~h},1,25\mathrm{~h},1.5\mathrm{~h},1.75\mathrm{~h},2\mathrm{~h},24\mathrm{~h}i$ and $48\mathrm{{h}}$ ), and the relative crystallinity is calculated from the ratio of the absorption peak area at $23.2^{\circ}$ of each sample to the $48\mathrm{~h~}$ sample. According to Fig S6a-b, all samples possess a standard absorption peaks of MFI topology structure. With the extension of crystallization time, the crystallinity of the obtained zeolite gradually increased from $60.59\%$ to $100\%$ . Combining with the SEM results, it can be concluded that the basic morphology of zeolite has been formed before complete crystallization.
Fig. 5j-q and Fig S5 shows the morphology evolution process of the MTS-SAC-0.025 zeolites obtained under different crystallization times. Meanwhile, the crystallization situations of samples under different crystallization times were further studied by powder XRD patterns (Fig S6c). During the first $1.5\mathrm{h}$ of crystallization, the amorphous precursor was gradually dissociated into many nano-scale granular fragments, and the detailed dissociation process is presented in Fig S5. With the crystallization time comes to $^{2\mathrm{~h~}}$ (Fig. 5j, n) and $^{6\mathrm{~h~}}$ (Fig. 5k, o), the small fragments begin to agglomerate and gradually form zeolite particles with a certain size. Further extending the crystallization time to $^{18\mathrm{~h~}}$ , the basic rough morphology of zeolite particles has already been constituted although the particles are still connected by edges (Fig. 5l, p). In the Fig. 5m, q, the morphology of obtained zeolites is basically consistent with MTS-SAC-0.025. From the Fig S6c, all samples crystallized within $1.5\mathrm{h}$ still exhibit an amorphous state, but with the extension of the crystallization time, the XRD curve of the sample gradually showed a wide absorption peak in the range of $7.2^{\circ}{-9.5^{\circ}}$ , which indicates that the crystallization has begun during this period. For the samples that crystallize ${>}2\mathrm{~h~}$ , the MFI topology structure have already formed. The relative crystallinity of those SAC samples was also calculated by referring to the above hydrothermal crystallization process (Fig S6d), and the value also increased with the crystallization time.
Based on the above characterized results, we proposed corresponding possible crystallization processes for two different processes in Fig. 5i and Fig. 5r. For the hydrothermal synthesis route: (i) the initial precursor solution evolves into many small nanocrystalline particles with a certain size, and the nucleation induction period was completed at this stage; (ii) undergoing the oriented aggregation of primary nanocrystalline particle; (iii) crystal growth and complete crystallization (Fig. 5i). For the SAC process, there is a certain difference in the crystallization process: (i) amorphous precursor powders (the XRD pattern is shown in Fig S6) are converted into nanocrystalline particles by a dissolution– recrystallization process upon steaming in the presence of $\mathrm{TPA}^{+}$ . During this step, the Ti species are introduced in the zeolite framework with a 4-coordination mode, and the nucleation induction period has been completed; (ii) Zeolite crystals gradually grow via the oriented aggregation of produced nanocrystallites (Fig. 5r). Comparing two processes, the difference in crystallization environment directly affects the microscopic morphology of the synthesized TS-1. Sufficient liquid phase components in the hydrothermal system provide a prerequisite for uniform crystallization of zeolite particles. But for the SAC route, due to the solid phase transformation mechanism is dominant during the whole crystallization process, resulting in a non-uniform zeolite particle size and the rough external surface.
Fig. 6a shows the possible mechanism for MOFs as dualfunctional template to induce synthesizing hierarchical TS-1. The generated Ti-OH after the structural destruction of MOFs can be directly used as titanium source to condense with surrounding Si-OH produced by TEOS hydrolysis, and the separated ligands take part in subsequent hierarchical pore structure building section. In detail, during the precursor hydrolysis stage, unlike TBOT directly hydrolyzing, Ti MOFs will gradually be destroyed in the strong alkaline environment, and only the exposed titanium can equip Ti-OH groups (Fig. 6b, Fig S7). This process effectively avoid the condensation between Ti-OH and resulting in more uniform distribution of titanium and silicon species in the obtained precursor gel before crystallization. Moreover, the decomposition of MOFs can also reduce the pH value of the system by consuming $\mathsf{O H}^{-}$ and slow down the crystallization rate. The above two aspects all help balance the forming rates of Ti-OH and Si-OH during crystallization, which are favorable to avoid the formation of anatase phase. As for the construction of hierarchical pore structure, some special interactions may exist between ligand components produced from the destruction of MOFs and crystallized nanoparticles. Such as the $\boldsymbol{-}\mathsf{N}\mathsf{H}_{2}$ in the ligands may form hydrogen bond with the hydroxyl group of the nanoparticle surface during the crystallization process, playing a role of fixing the ligands (Fig. 6a). Subsequently the bulk hydrophobic functional groups containing aromatic ring of anchored ligands maybe form the organic layer to generate uniform small size intracrystalline mesopores after calcination, just like the role of Triton X-100 mentioned in the literature[47]. In addition, some intercrystalline mesopores can be generated in the process of nanoparticle accumulation and ostwald ripening, along with the produced micropores oriented by $\mathrm{TPA}^{+}$ , the hierarchical pore structure of zeolite is constructed.
## 3.3. Catalytic performance for oxidation of 1-hexene
The catalytic performances of the prepared MTS, MTS-SAC and TS-C samples were tested by the oxidation of 1 hexene with hydrogen peroxide as the oxidant. The results (Table 2) show that the TSC has the lowest substrate conversion $(25.7\ \%)$ and selectivity of epoxidation products $(>96.0\ \%)$ compared to other hierarchical TS-1 samples. As for MTS samples, the increased titanium content does not seem to be able to effectively improve the performance of the catalyst. From the two aspects of catalytic performance and preparation cost, MTS-0.025 should be the most suitable catalyst for the oxidation of 1-hexene. In the reaction environment, the target product 1,2 epoxyhexane can be converted into other byproducts such as valeraldehyde and 1,2-hexanediol through subsequent reactions. Many studies have proven that the improved mass transfer effect of catalyst contributes to increasing the selectivity of target products[22,24,25]. Since the construction of the hierarchical pore structure is beneficial to improve the mass transfer effect of substances, and all MTS samples have a higher epoxidation products selectivity and 1 hexene conversion than TS-C, suggesting from the side that MOFs as titanium sources are conducive to the formation of hierarchical pores through the hydrothermal synthesis process. In the case of MTS-SAC samples, all the zeolites also perform a favorable selectivity of $>99\%$ , although the SAC samples generally have a minor total pore volume than hydrothermal samples, and the conversion of the 1 hexene is basically keeping constant $(30.5-33.9\ \%)$ . The possible reason is that the SAC sample has a relatively high framework titanium content, which can be reflected by the larger I960/I800 value in the FT-IR spectrum of MTS-SAC (1.45–1.97 vs 1.94–2.27). Table S2 lists the catalytic performance data for 1 hexene epoxidation of reported TS-1 samples. By comparing with Table 2, it can be seen that the MTS and MTSSAC series samples synthesized by the MOFs-induced strategy have surpassed most of the reported TS-1 zeolites in terms of substrate conversion. As for the selectivity of target product, the samples synthesized in this work also showed a great advantage, which can achieve the complete selective conversion of 1 hexene to the target products.
In order to verify the stability of the obtained MTS and MTS-SAC catalysts, the recycling experiments of 1 hexene epoxidation were performed with MTS-0.025 and MTS-SAC-0.025. As shown in Fig. 7a, all two kinds of prepared hierarchical zeolite have a good recyclability. With the catalyst was used for 4 times, the conversion of the reaction substrate decreases slightly, but it can still be maintained at about $30\%$ for MTS-0.025 and about $26\%$ for MTSSAC-0.025, respectively, and the selectivity of epoxidation product keeps $599\%$ . The SEM, FT-IR and XRD analyses are used to further determine the stability of recovered two zeolites which have gone through 4 cycles, and the relevant data are compared with the corresponding initial TS-1. According to Fig. 7b, Fig. 3a and Fig. 3i, the morphology and particle size of two recycled samples are basically unchanged. In Fig. 7c-d, the FT-IR spectra and XRD patterns of recycled catalysts do not show an obvious peak position change, indicating that the chemical composition of the material also does not change significantly before and after the reaction. Overall, all the above results can demonstrate that the prepared MTS and MTS-SAC zeolites have a favorable stability.
## 4. Conclusions
In summary, we first propose a new anatase-free hierarchical titanium silicalite-1 synthetic strategy which uses NH2-MIL-125 as Ti source and TPAOH as sole template agent under hydrothermal crystallization or steam assisted crystallization conditions. Detailed characterization results demonstrated resultant TS-1 catalysts all have no catalytic reaction-unfavorable anatase phase, because the use of MOFs can balance the forming rates of Ti-OH and Si-OH. And considerable amount of inter-crystalline mesopores with pore size of $30{-}50~\mathrm{nm}$ were proven existing inside the zeolite by BET pore size analysis, owing to the ligands in MOFs can act as template to construct the hierarchical pore structure. The catalytic performances of the obtained MTS and MTS-SAC samples were tested with the 1 hexene epoxidation reaction, and all those samples exhibited excellent catalytic performance and epoxidation products selectivity than conventional TS-1. Furthermore, the two series of HTS all has a favorable recyclability and can maintain its catalytic performance for at least four complete cycles. More efforts such as adjusting TPAOH amount and the ratio of water to silicon are still needed to further improve the performance of catalyst, this would be very meaningful in the preparation of more efficient alkene epoxidation catalysts for industrial applications.
